<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title><%= locals.documentTitle || title %></title>
  <link rel="preload" href="/fonts/Bevellier-Variable.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
  <link rel="preload" href="/fonts/Supreme-Variable.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/output.css">
  <script src="/vendor/gsap/gsap.min.js" defer></script>
  <script src="/vendor/gsap/SplitText.min.js" defer></script>
  <script src="/vendor/gsap/ScrollTrigger.min.js" defer></script>
  <script src="/vendor/iconify-icon.min.js" defer></script>
  <script src="/js/loader.js" defer></script>
  <script src="/js/scroll-reveal.js" defer></script>
  <noscript><style>#loader { display: none; }</style></noscript>
</head>
<body class="flex min-h-dvh flex-col antialiased">
  <!-- Loading screen: outlined wordmark that fills as the page loads, then slides up into the nav (public/js/loader.js). -->
  <div id="loader" aria-hidden="true" class="fixed inset-0 z-50 flex items-center justify-center bg-white">
    <div id="loader-word" class="relative font-bevellier text-8xl font-semibold leading-none tracking-tight opacity-0">
      <span class="block [-webkit-text-fill-color:transparent] [-webkit-text-stroke:1px_currentColor]">.trifoldi</span>
      <span id="loader-fill" class="absolute inset-0 block" style="clip-path: inset(100% 0 0 0)">.trifoldi</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Hero screen: the nav plus <main>. A wrapper, rather than flex-1 on <main> alone filling whatever's left
       in <body>, so the footer below (a second full screen in its own right, see its comment) can size itself
       independently instead of competing with <main> for the same leftover space — see the footer's comment
       for why that competition used to matter here.
       The headline sits a little above the middle of the screen, and the footer follows it up by the same
       amount, so the gap between the two is what it would be with the headline centred. Both come from one
       value, --hero-lift: the wrapper is that much short of a full screen, so the footer starts that much
       higher and its eyebrow (flush at its top) peeks in at the bottom of the first screen, and <main> gives
       the same amount back as bottom padding, so the headline, centred in what is left, rises by the whole
       lift rather than half of it. Change --hero-lift to move both together; 0 centres the headline in a full
       screen again, with the footer starting just below it. -->
  <div class="flex min-h-[calc(100dvh_-_var(--hero-lift))] flex-col [--hero-lift:10dvh]">
  <!-- The <nav> sets the side margins and the inner box holds the row plus the line under it, so the line
       always spans the same width as the content. Each link sits in its own <li>; public/js/loader.js
       slides the links up out of these containers and grows the line from the middle out to the sides. -->
  <nav aria-label="Main" class="px-6 lg:px-20">
    <div class="relative grid grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_auto_minmax(0,1fr)] items-center py-4">
      <ul data-nav-links="left" class="hidden items-center gap-9 justify-self-start lg:flex">
        <li><a href="/motion" class="block font-semibold uppercase">Motion</a></li>
        <li><a href="/design" class="block font-semibold uppercase">Design</a></li>
        <li><a href="/lab" class="block font-semibold uppercase">Lab</a></li>
      </ul>
      <a id="nav-mark" href="/" class="col-start-2 font-bevellier text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">.trifoldi</a>
      <ul data-nav-links="right" class="hidden items-center gap-9 justify-self-end lg:flex">
        <li><a href="/about" class="block font-semibold uppercase">About</a></li>
        <li><a href="/contest" class="block font-semibold uppercase">Contest</a></li>
        <li><a href="/career" class="block font-semibold uppercase">Career</a></li>
      </ul>
      <span id="nav-line" aria-hidden="true" class="absolute inset-x-0 bottom-0 h-[2px] bg-alto-300"></span>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <!-- Headline: one <h1>, two lines, set in Supreme ExtraBold (the mockup's headline is Supreme, not the
       Bevellier h1 default). Sized to the viewport width, as in the mockup (150px at 1920), and capped at that
       size. Weight, size and tracking were fitted to the mockup's measured letter shapes and line widths.
       The <body> is a full-screen column: the hero (the wrapper around this nav and <main>, see its comment
       above), then the footer, a second full screen in its own right (see its comment). <main> is flex-1
       within the hero wrapper and a column, so it centres the headline and the project info row under it (see
       the info's comment, after the <h1>) as one block in whatever's left after the nav and its own bottom
       padding (the lift, see the wrapper's comment). In a window too short for the nav, headline and strip
       together, the wrapper just grows past its budget to fit them, with the headline right under the nav and
       the lift still below it.
       Each line is a row: the text, then a round outline button after its last word (a plus on the top line, an
       external-link arrow on the bottom one). The buttons sit beside the text spans, not inside them, so the
       letter split in public/js/loader.js never touches them; it also shows them once the letters have landed.
       Their size, gap and drop from the line's top are in em, so they follow the fluid headline size; only the
       size stops shrinking (at 1.75rem) so they stay usable on a phone. The <h1> is named from the two text
       spans, which keeps the buttons and the photos out of its accessible name.
       The two lines of text sit one on top of the other, and the photo strip is a third row under them, inside
       the <h1> so the block stays centred as one. It is decorative (empty alt) and full-bleed: the negative side
       margins cancel <main>'s padding (keep them equal to it) and the fourth photo runs off the right edge, as
       in the mockup. Sizes are the mockup's at 1920 (photos 523 x 274, gaps 21, 36 from the text's letters to
       the strip) in vw, so they scale with the headline and 3.5 photos show at any width. The 0.09em top margin
       is what is left of that 36 once the bottom line box's own 22px under the letters is counted.
       These classes are the strip's resting state. public/js/loader.js collapses every photo's wrapper to a
       2px line, so the text sits on its own as it starts to animate, then grows the wrappers back to
       their full height as soon as the top line has landed, each on its own timing. A wrapper is one of the
       rectangles: it clips its photo (overflow-clip) and keeps it centred (items-center), so the photo is
       uncovered from the middle outwards as the wrapper grows. The row is as tall as its tallest wrapper, and
       it takes real room in the flow, so <main>'s centring pushes the two lines up as it grows.
       overflow-x-clip on the row is only for the fourth photo running off the right edge.
       The photos sit in one track, which takes over the row's gap and adds one more after the last photo
       (padding), so the track is exactly one cycle of the strip wide: photo, gap, photo, gap, ... From the
       viewer's side nothing changes. public/js/loader.js lays a clone of the track (aria-hidden) beside it
       as the page loads and scrolls both by one track width, right to left, for ever, from the start (the
       reveal included). The clone carries on where the original ends, so the jump back at the end of each cycle isn't visible. The gaps
       and widths are all in vw, so a track is always wider than the screen and two are enough at any size.
       The scroll stands still while the pointer is over a photo (listeners on the row, so the clone counts),
       and a round button with an arrow (data-strip-cursor, at the end of the <body>) takes the pointer's place.
       The strip can also be scrolled by hand (a wheel flick, a swipe or a drag along the row, see
       public/js/loader.js). The row is touch-pan-y, so a finger moving along it belongs to the strip while one
       moving up and down still scrolls the page, and select-none, so a drag doesn't select anything.
       A tiny round button under the strip's right end (data-strip-toggle) pauses and resumes the strip's own
       scroll; scrolling by hand still works while it is paused. It is at the right end of the project info row,
       level with the project's name (see the info's comment, after the <h1>). The button is hidden (the
       attribute) until public/js/loader.js finds there is a scroll to pause, so without the script or with reduced
       motion it isn't there, and the script pops it in once the strip is open. It has two icons, the pause (IconMind)
       and a play triangle drawn to match it, because the pack has no plain one (24 grid, stroke 1.5, round ends);
       the script shows one at a time by their hidden attribute and updates the button's label.
       The photos rest in grayscale and get their colours back while the pointer is over them, and while the photo
       is the active project's (data-active on the wrapper, see the project info's comment after the <h1>; the
       wrapper is the `group`, so the <img> reads it through group-data-active). It is a filter on
       the <img> itself, and the transition covers only the filter (transition-[filter], not the plain
       transition), because public/js/loader.js tweens the same <img>'s opacity for the reveal and a CSS
       transition on opacity would lag behind it. The clone copies these classes, so it behaves the same.
       Once the strip scrolls, public/js/loader.js also draws every <img> a fifth bigger than its box and slides it
       about inside, for a parallax (stripParallax there): the box's own overflow-clip hides what sticks out, the
       layout is untouched (it is a transform) and the transition doesn't cover it, as it doesn't cover opacity.
       With reduced motion, where the strip never scrolls, the photos keep their own size.
       Each photo also carries a caption, its project's name with its category under it, laid over the bottom-left
       corner of its own box: while the pointer is over the photo, a dark gradient fades in, the name's letters rise
       one after another out of a mask (in Bevellier) and the category rises after them, as a line. The markup is
       views/partials/strip-caption.ejs (its comment has the details), included once per photo with `name` and
       `category`. The same two are on the wrapper as data-project-name and data-project-category, for the project
       info under the strip (the clone copies them too). It is the wrapper's second child and takes no pointer events, so the <img> underneath keeps the
       hover (and its grayscale filter) and the scroll's hover check still finds the photo. The wrapper is the
       `group` that drives it, so the clone gets it too, it moves with its photo, and it is clipped with it while
       the strip opens. The captions are text, read once (the clone is aria-hidden) and, like the photos, outside
       the <h1>'s accessible name. As it is a sibling of the <img>, not a child, it stays put in the box while the
       photo slides under it.
       The photos are the project covers in assets/project_public_covers (ppc001 to ppc004), served from that
       folder itself at /project_public_covers (a static mount in server.js), with no copy in public/. The list,
       in order, with each cover's file, name and category, is `projects` in routes/public.js; the strip is one
       photo per entry. Photos are cropped to the frame by object-cover, from the middle.
       A click on a photo opens it: it grows from its place in the strip to fill the screen (see the expander at the end
       of the <body>) and stays there until it is clicked or Escape is pressed, when it shrinks back. The photos are not
       links yet, so that is all a click does; it is the effect the page transitions will use. -->
  <main class="flex flex-1 flex-col justify-center px-6 pb-(--hero-lift) lg:px-20">
    <h1 aria-labelledby="headline-top headline-bottom" class="w-full font-supreme font-extrabold uppercase leading-none tracking-tighter [font-size:min(7.8vw,10rem)]">
      <span class="flex items-start">
        <span id="headline-top" data-headline-line>Where <span class="text-alto-400">Ideas</span></span>
        <button type="button" data-headline-button aria-label="Show more" class="ml-[0.3em] mt-[0.18em] flex size-[max(0.375em,1.75rem)] items-center justify-center rounded-full border border-alto-400 text-alto-400 cursor-pointer transition-colors hover:border-alto-700 hover:text-alto-700">
          <iconify-icon icon="iconmind:plus-outline-thin" class="[font-size:max(0.165em,0.77rem)]"></iconify-icon>
        </button>
      </span>
      <span class="flex items-start">
        <span id="headline-bottom" data-headline-line>meet <span class="text-alto-400">expectations</span></span>
        <button type="button" data-headline-button aria-label="Open" class="ml-[0.3em] mt-[0.18em] flex size-[max(0.375em,1.75rem)] items-center justify-center rounded-full border border-alto-400 text-alto-400 cursor-pointer transition-colors hover:border-alto-700 hover:text-alto-700">
          <iconify-icon icon="iconmind:arrow-external-outline-thin" class="[font-size:max(0.165em,0.77rem)]"></iconify-icon>
        </button>
      </span>
      <span data-strip class="-mx-6 mt-[0.09em] flex touch-pan-y select-none items-center overflow-x-clip lg:-mx-20">
        <span data-strip-track class="flex shrink-0 items-center gap-[1.09vw] pr-[1.09vw]">
          <% projects.forEach((project, i) => { %>
          <span data-strip-photo data-project-name="<%= project.name %>" data-project-category="<%= project.category %>" <%= i === 0 ? 'data-active' : '' %> class="group relative flex shrink-0 items-center overflow-clip">
            <img src="/project_public_covers/<%= project.cover %>" alt="" class="block aspect-[523/274] w-[27.24vw] object-cover grayscale transition-[filter] duration-500 hover:grayscale-0 group-data-active:grayscale-0">
            <%- include('../partials/strip-caption', { name: project.name, category: project.category }) %>
          </span>
          <% }) %>
        </span>
      </span>
    </h1>

    <!-- Project info: the name and category of the strip's active project, on the left under the strip, with the
         pause button (see the strip's comment) at the other end of the row. It doesn't move with the strip. A
         photo becomes active when its left edge reaches an imaginary vertical line just before the headline's text,
         which is this row's own left edge, and stays active until the next photo's left edge gets there: the active
         one is the photo with the nearest left edge at or past the line. It is the one in colour (data-active, see
         the strip's comment). public/js/loader.js (stripInfo there) works this out
         every frame while the strip scrolls and, when it changes, slides the name and category up out of their masks
         and brings the new ones up from below, the name letter by letter and the category as a line, as the photos'
         captions do; at load they come in as the strip finishes opening. The name is read from a visually hidden copy
         (data-strip-info-label), since the script splits the visible one into letters (aria-hidden). It isn't a live
         region: it changes every few seconds by itself, which would be read out over and over.
         The server renders the project that is active with the strip at rest, which is what stays with reduced motion
         or without the script: the strip starts with the first photo at the screen's left edge, already at or past
         the line, so that is the first project, and its photo is the one marked data-active.
         The type follows the photos' captions (views/partials/strip-caption.ejs): the name in Bevellier at the
         photo cursor's fluid size, the category in Supreme at half that but never below text-xs, here in the page's
         colours rather than white. The category doesn't wrap, so a long one can't change the row's height and shift
         the centred block. The row sets the name's size, so the pause button can sit level with the name's line box
         (1.2em) through its top margin, in em. -->
    <% const restingProject = projects[0]; %>
    <div class="mt-[max(0.75rem,1.25vw)] flex items-start justify-between gap-6 [font-size:clamp(0.9rem,1.5vw,1.9rem)]">
      <p data-strip-info class="uppercase tracking-wide">
        <span class="block overflow-clip whitespace-nowrap font-bevellier font-semibold leading-[1.2]">
          <span data-strip-info-label class="sr-only"><%= restingProject.name %></span>
          <span data-strip-info-name aria-hidden="true" class="block"><%= restingProject.name %></span>
        </span>
        <span class="block overflow-clip">
          <span data-strip-info-category class="block whitespace-nowrap font-supreme font-medium leading-[1.3] text-alto-500 [font-size:max(var(--text-xs),0.5em)]"><%= restingProject.category %></span>
        </span>
      </p>
      <button type="button" data-strip-toggle hidden aria-label="Pause the photo strip" class="mt-[calc((1.2em_-_1.5rem)/2)] flex size-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full border border-alto-400 text-alto-400 cursor-pointer transition-colors hover:border-alto-700 hover:text-alto-700">
        <iconify-icon data-strip-toggle-pause icon="iconmind:pause-outline-thin" class="text-[0.95rem]"></iconify-icon>
        <svg data-strip-toggle-play hidden xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" class="size-[0.95rem]"><path d="M8 4.5v15l12-7.5Z"/></svg>
      </button>
    </div>
  </main>
  </div>

  <!-- Footer: the last row of the <body> column (see the hero wrapper above). Its previous design (a divider line
       mirroring the nav's, this paragraph, and an Inquiries/Question/Socials column list) was dropped for a
       revamp; the paragraph is the first piece back, with a new scroll-driven colour reveal in place of the
       old load-triggered slide-up. It starts in a muted alto shade and its words turn to the brand's darkest
       shade (bunker-950) one after another as the paragraph passes through the viewport (data-scroll-reveal,
       public/js/scroll-reveal.js), which runs independently of loader.js's load sequence, so it plays whenever
       the user actually scrolls this far rather than at page load. The old divider line and the right-hand
       columns remain dropped, pending their own redesign; the "who are we" eyebrow (a small-caps label plus a
       line filling the rest of the row, unrelated to that old divider) now sits above the paragraph instead,
       on <footer>'s own px-6 lg:px-20 — the same width as the nav, not the paragraph's own 10%-inset column
       below it. Lower-case in the markup, not "Who": font-variant-caps only shrinks letters that were
       actually lower-case, so a capital W would stay full height and read as a mismatched size next to the
       small caps beside it.
       The eyebrow peeks in at the bottom of the first screen (see the hero wrapper's comment), so it comes in
       with the nav at load: public/js/loader.js grows its line (#footer-line) the way it grows the nav's and
       slides its label (data-footer-item) up out of its row.
       <footer> is min-h-[200dvh]: one screen for the section's own content, then a second, empty screen's
       worth of extra scroll room behind it purely so the reveal has somewhere to happen "after" the section
       has fully arrived, not while it's still scrolling into place — the reveal is meant to wait for that.
       GSAP's ScrollTrigger pin (which freezes an element in place via a JS-inserted spacer, synthesizing this
       same kind of room) was tried twice for this and both times the reveal simply never animated, for a
       reason that couldn't be pinned down without a live browser to inspect — position: sticky, a plain CSS
       feature with nothing GSAP-specific about how it holds an element in place, is used here instead: the
       inner div (min-h-dvh flex-col, everything the section actually shows) is sticky top-0, so it scrolls
       normally until its top reaches the top of the viewport and then holds there — visually frozen, same as
       a pin would have looked — for exactly as long as <footer>'s extra screen of height beneath it lasts,
       then releases and scrolls away normally as <footer> runs out. public/js/scroll-reveal.js's own comment
       covers how the reveal's timing maps onto that held period.
       Within the inner div, only the paragraph's own wrapper is flex-1 items-center: the eyebrow stays flush
       at the top on its own pt-11, fixed there regardless of what the paragraph does below it — centring the
       two as one group, tried briefly, tied the eyebrow's position to the paragraph's height, which wasn't
       wanted. The paragraph then centres in whatever's left under the eyebrow, the same pattern <main> above
       uses to centre the headline in whatever's left after the nav.
       leading-[1] and tracking-[-0.06em] on the paragraph itself are taken from the same values on
       nexstudio.tech's .about__copy, the site this section is modelled on. That page also caps .about__copy's
       own width at 16.5ch, for short, punchy line breaks proportional to whatever font-size it ends up at
       instead of a fixed rem or vw one — left off here, since the paragraph is meant to fill the full width of
       its own 10%-inset column (10-80-10), not a narrower measure centred within it.
       The line breaks inside the text are manual, four words each, rather than a width cap: this sentence's
       words vary too much in length (4 to 13 characters) for any single ch or vw measure to hold every line at
       four words without sometimes wrapping a fifth short one in or forcing a long one down to three early, so
       a width answers "how wide" but not reliably "how many" — SplitText's word-by-word colour reveal works
       the same regardless of where the breaks fall.
       Each line is now a real, hand-authored pair of nested <span>s rather than a plain line of text ended
       with a <br>: the outer one (block, overflow-hidden) is the static mask, the inner one (data-line-reveal)
       is what public/js/scroll-reveal.js slides up out of it, the same technique loader.js already uses for
       the nav's own links — SplitText's own line-splitting was tried here three separate times first (once
       with its mask option, once building a mask by hand around lines it detected) and broke the paragraph
       every time in a way that was never fully explained; hand-authoring the lines directly sidesteps needing
       SplitText to detect them at all. SplitText's word split for the colour reveal still runs on the whole
       <p> as before and finds every word regardless of this extra nesting. Retune the breaks themselves, by
       moving words between spans, if the copy changes.
       Behind the paragraph sits a ghost grid of square cells (data-cell-grid), empty here and filled in by
       public/js/scroll-reveal.js, which picks how many columns and rows fit so the cells are exact squares that
       span the same width as the eyebrow's line. Each cell starts with a faint alto border and switches to a darker
       one in reading order off the same scroll progress as the words, so the two finish together. The grid is
       absolutely positioned inside the paragraph's wrapper (which is now relative) so it never affects layout, and
       the paragraph is relative too, which is all it takes to paint above it. The grid is decorative and, like the
       reveal itself, only exists when the script runs (nothing without JS or with reduced motion). Once every
       cell has been highlighted, on a device with a hovering, precise pointer, the cells near the cursor also
       fill with a faint tint that fades out behind it, and a click or tap on a cell sets off a ripple, a ring
       expanding from it across the grid that tints the cells it passes and shrinks them briefly (a transform:
       scale, so nothing around them moves). Neither does anything before that, while the reveal is still under
       way. The grid takes no pointer events (the paragraph is above it), so the script listens on <footer> and
       works out the cell from the position. -->
  <footer class="min-h-[200dvh] px-6 lg:px-20">
    <div class="sticky top-0 flex min-h-dvh flex-col">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 pt-11">
        <span data-footer-item class="shrink-0 text-sm font-medium tracking-wide text-alto-500 [font-variant:small-caps]">who are we</span>
        <span id="footer-line" aria-hidden="true" class="h-[2px] flex-1 bg-alto-300"></span>
      </div>
      <div class="relative flex flex-1 items-center px-[10%]">
        <div data-cell-grid aria-hidden="true" class="pointer-events-none absolute left-0 top-1/2 grid -translate-y-1/2"></div>
        <p data-scroll-reveal class="relative text-9xl font-extrabold leading-[1] tracking-[-0.06em]"><span class="block overflow-hidden"><span data-line-reveal class="block">Dedicated expert teams which</span></span><span class="block overflow-hidden"><span data-line-reveal class="block">mission is to bring</span></span><span class="block overflow-hidden"><span data-line-reveal class="block">your visionary concept ideas</span></span><span class="block overflow-hidden"><span data-line-reveal class="block">into tangible realities with</span></span><span class="block overflow-hidden"><span data-line-reveal class="block">unparalleled quality and innovation.</span></span></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </footer>

  <!-- Floating contact button: a single round icon button pinned to the bottom-right corner. From lg up it is as far
       from the bottom as from the right (5rem), which lifts it clear of the footer's last line. -->
  <a href="/contact" aria-label="Contact" class="fixed bottom-6 right-6 z-40 flex size-14 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-bunker-950 text-bunker-50 lg:bottom-20 lg:right-20">
    <iconify-icon icon="iconmind:agent-listen-outline-thin" class="text-2xl"></iconify-icon>
  </a>

  <!-- Scroll toast: a small dark pill at the bottom of the screen, in the middle, that says to keep scrolling. It
       is up while the footer's paragraph is held in place and still being revealed (public/js/scroll-reveal.js
       shows it as the section arrives and takes it away again on scrolling back up out of the section, or on
       reaching the end of the reveal, when there is nothing left to scroll for). It is the same height as the
       contact button and sits on the same line as it, so the two are level. It rests invisible and the script
       is what shows it, so without the script, or with reduced motion (no reveal to scroll through), it never
       appears. Decorative and pointer-transparent: the text is only a hint at what the scroll does, and
       everything it points at is readable without it. The arrow bobs to point the way. -->
  <div aria-hidden="true" class="pointer-events-none fixed inset-x-0 bottom-6 z-40 flex h-14 items-center justify-center lg:bottom-20">
    <span data-scroll-toast class="invisible flex items-center gap-2 rounded-full bg-bunker-950 py-3 pl-5 pr-4 text-sm font-medium text-bunker-50">
      keep scrolling
      <iconify-icon icon="iconmind:arrow-down-outline-thin" class="text-base"></iconify-icon>
    </span>
  </div>

  <!-- Photo cursor: a white round button with an arrow pointing down and to the right, which stands in for the
       pointer while it is over one of the strip's photos. public/js/loader.js hides the real cursor on the photos,
       keeps this button centred on the pointer and scales it in and out as the pointer moves onto and off a photo.
       It is decorative and takes no pointer events, so the photo underneath still gets them and the scroll's
       hover check sees the photo, not the button. It rests invisible, and the script is what shows it and what
       hides the real cursor, so without the script, with reduced motion, or on a screen with no hovering pointer
       nobody is left without a cursor. Its size follows one fluid font size, as the headline's buttons do: the
       circle is 2.5em wide and the arrow 1em, so both scale with the window, between 1.25rem (small window) and
       1.9rem (large screen) of font size. -->
  <span data-strip-cursor aria-hidden="true" class="pointer-events-none invisible fixed left-0 top-0 z-50 flex size-[2.5em] items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-white text-bunker-950 [font-size:clamp(1.25rem,1.5vw,1.9rem)]">
    <iconify-icon icon="iconmind:arrow-down-right-outline-thin"></iconify-icon>
  </span>

  <!-- Card expander: what a clicked photo turns into as it grows to fill the screen (public/js/loader.js, stripExpand).
       It is a copy of the photo, not the photo itself: the photos sit in the scrolling tracks, whose transforms would
       make a fixed-position photo relative to its track instead of the screen, and in a row that clips. The script sets
       the picture, the box and the parallax from the photo that was clicked, lays this exactly over that photo and grows
       it to the screen, so the hand-over is not visible. It rests invisible and the script is what shows it, so without
       the script nothing covers the page. It is above everything (z-70: the loader and the photo cursor are z-50, the
       nav's wordmark flies at z-60), takes pointer events while it shows, so that a click on it closes it, and is
       decorative: the photos are not links yet, so there is nothing for assistive tech to announce. -->
  <div data-strip-expander aria-hidden="true" class="invisible fixed inset-0 z-[70] cursor-zoom-out overflow-hidden">
    <img alt="" draggable="false" class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 block size-full select-none object-cover">
  </div>
</body>
</html>
